      {
        "id": "alg-qc-state-preparation",
        "kind": "algorithm",
        "title": "QC24 State Preparation",
        "deps": [
          "def-qc-state-preparation-cost-model",
          "def-qc-one-qubit-gate-alphabet-and-rotations",
          "def-qc-controlled-unitary-access",
          "def-qc-pauli-matrices",
          "def-qc-ordered-bitstring-encoding",
          "def-qc-toffoli-and-fredkin",
          "thm-qc-axis-rotation-pauli-formula",
          "def-qc-circuit-denotational-semantics",
          "thm-polar-form-with-unique-principal-argument",
          "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity"
        ],
        "dependency_level": 10,
        "statement": "**Input and output.** Input an integer $n\\ge0$ and an explicit exact table $(a_x)_{x\\in\\{0,1\\}^n}$ in the ordered basis convention of [[def-qc-ordered-bitstring-encoding]]. If the table length is not $D=2^n$ or $\\sum_x|a_x|^2\\ne1$, return INVALID. Otherwise return a finite continuous-gate circuit which prepares the ray $[a]$ from $|0^n\\rangle$, together with its classical angle table. When the table is supplied as a dense fixed-width bit encoding, read all $b$ bits of each real and imaginary coordinate before computation. Arithmetic is ideal and exact: it supplies complex arithmetic, absolute value, exact zero tests and square roots; for $x,y\\ge0$ not both zero, $\\operatorname{atan2}(y,x)$ returns the unique $\\phi\\in[0,\\pi/2]$ with $\\cos\\phi=x/\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$ and $\\sin\\phi=y/\\sqrt{x^2+y^2}$. For $z\\ne0$, $\\arg z\\in(-\\pi,\\pi]$ is the unique principal angle in the polar form of [[thm-polar-form-with-unique-principal-argument]], so $z=|z|e^{i\\arg z}$ by [[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]]. No Turing bit-time bound is asserted.\n\n**Exact gate-family operation model.** Interpret this procedure's output in the ideal continuous-angle model with exact one-qubit gates $R_y(\\theta)$ and $P_\\phi:=\\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{i\\phi})$ for arbitrary real $\\theta,\\phi$, the two-qubit gate $C(R_y(\\theta))=\\operatorname{diag}(I_2,R_y(\\theta))$ with the control first, and the fixed gates $X$ ([[def-qc-pauli-matrices]]) and Toffoli ([[def-qc-toffoli-and-fredkin]]). Each occurrence of any of these gates costs one gate occurrence; the operation model explicitly grants controlled access to every $R_y(\\theta)$ ([[def-qc-controlled-unitary-access]]). The phase gate is unitary since $|e^{i\\phi}|=1$ ([[cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity]]). This is an ideal parameterized gate-family model, distinct from a finite fixed-matrix alphabet. It does not assert that arbitrary-angle gates are exactly available from a finite alphabet or give their finite-alphabet cost.\n\n**Prefix norms and rotations.** For every binary prefix $p$ of length at most $n$, set\n\n$$r_p=\\sqrt{\\sum_{x\\text{ extends }p}|a_x|^2}.$$\n\nFor $\\theta\\in\\mathbb R$ define\n\n$$R_y(\\theta)=\\begin{pmatrix}\\cos(\\theta/2)&-\\sin(\\theta/2)\\\\\\sin(\\theta/2)&\\cos(\\theta/2)\\end{pmatrix}$$\n\nin basis $(|0\\rangle,|1\\rangle)$. This is the $y$-axis rotation in the QC07 exponent convention; [[thm-qc-axis-rotation-pauli-formula]] gives this matrix and its unitarity. Define $C(U)=\\operatorname{diag}(I_2,U)$ with the control as the first wire, following [[def-qc-controlled-unitary-access]]. For each $l=0,\\ldots,n-1$ and each length-$l$ prefix $p$ in lexicographic order, skip the operation if $r_p=0$; otherwise set $\\theta_p=2\\operatorname{atan2}(r_{p1},r_{p0})$ and apply $R_y(\\theta_p)$ to bit $l$, controlled on the first $l$ bits equaling $p$. Length zero means no control. Thus no division is performed on a zero-norm branch.\n\n**Exact bounded-arity control construction.** For a prefix rotation with $m\\ge2$ control bits, conjugate each zero-control bit by $X$ so the requested condition becomes all ones. Allocate $m-1$ clean work bits $w_1,\\ldots,w_{m-1}$. Compute $w_1=c_1c_2$ with one Toffoli; for $j=3,\\ldots,m$, compute $w_{j-1}=w_{j-2}c_j$ with one Toffoli. Apply $C(R_y(\\theta_p))$ to the flag and target bits, with the flag as the control, then apply the Toffolis in reverse order and undo the $X$ conjugations. For $m=1$, apply $C(R_y(\\theta_p))$ directly when the requested pattern is $1$; for pattern $0$, conjugate that controlled gate by $X$ on its control wire. For $m=0$, apply $R_y(\\theta_p)$ directly to the target bit. For a leaf phase with $m\\ge2$, use the same flag construction and apply $P_\\phi=\\operatorname{diag}(1,e^{i\\phi})$ to the final work bit instead of a controlled rotation. A one-bit leaf phase uses $P_\\phi$ directly for pattern $1$ and $XP_\\phi X$ for pattern $0$. The work bits return to zero after each operation and are reused; no multi-control gate is assumed as a primitive.\n\n**Leaf phases.** For a basis string $x$, define $P_{x,\\phi}=I+(e^{i\\phi}-1)|x\\rangle\\langle x|$. Let $x_*$ be the lexicographically first index with $a_{x_*}\\ne0$. Put $\\phi_*=\\arg(a_{x_*})$. For each other $x$ with $a_x\\ne0$, put $\\phi_x=\\arg(a_x)-\\phi_*$ and, unless $\\phi_x\\equiv0\\pmod{2\\pi}$, apply $P_{x,\\phi_x}$ using the control construction above. The reference leaf needs no phase, and zero entries and zero relative phases require no gate. Every nonzero output amplitude is then $e^{-i\\phi_*}a_x$, including the reference leaf, so the output is the target ray. When $n=0$, the valid table has one unit-modulus entry and the empty circuit already prepares its unique ray.",
        "justified_by": [
          "thm-qc-state-preparation-rotation-level-upper-bound"
        ],
